Change implementation steps
1. Preparation and impact assessment
1.	Log in to the CyberArk Technical Community and review CA26-36. 
2.	Record the fixed version required for each component: 
o	TPC: <CA26-36 fixed version> 
o	Microsoft Windows Local with WMI: <CA26-36 fixed version> 
o	Database Credentials Management Framework: <CA26-36 fixed version> 
3.	Download the packages directly from CyberArk Marketplace. 
4.	Review each package’s release notes and installation instructions. 
5.	Confirm compatibility with the installed CPM and Privilege Cloud Connector version. 
6.	Identify: 
o	Every CPM server. 
o	Platforms assigned to each CPM. 
o	Platforms using TPC. 
o	Platforms using Windows Local with WMI. 
o	Database String and other platforms using the Database Credentials Management Framework. 
7.	Identify any custom process, prompts, INI, XML, command or DLL files. 
8.	Select representative non-production test accounts for each plugin type. 
TPC platforms can be identified by reviewing Automatic Password Management > CPM Plug-in and checking whether ExeName is configured as CyberArk.TPC.exe. 
2. Backup
Before making changes:
1.	Export the existing affected platforms from Platform Management. 
2.	Back up the CPM plugin directories and relevant files from the CPM installation path, normally under: 
C:\Program Files (x86)\CyberArk\Password Manager\bin
3.	Specifically back up: 
o	TPC executable and supporting libraries. 
o	WMI plugin files. 
o	WMICommandStmt.txt. 
o	WMIReconcileCommandStmt.txt. 
o	Database framework files. 
o	Custom process and prompts files. 
4.	Capture existing file versions, timestamps and SHA-256 hashes. 
5.	Record the current CyberArk Password Manager service status and CPM health. 
Do not back up or copy the entire CPM installation over the patched installation during rollback; preserve only the files specifically being changed.
3. Test the packages in non-production
1.	Import the remediated packages into the non-production Privilege Cloud/PVWA environment. 
2.	Confirm that platform customizations remain intact. 
3.	Confirm that the patched files are deployed to every non-production CPM. 
4.	Restart the CPM service where required by the package instructions. 
5.	Test: 
o	TPC Verify, Change and Reconcile. 
o	Windows Local WMI Change and Reconcile. 
o	Database credential update against a test database. 
6.	Review the CPM and plugin logs for errors. 
7.	Confirm that the target and vaulted passwords remain synchronized. 
The Database Credentials Management Framework is used by the Accounts Stored in Databases/Database String functionality. CyberArk documentation states that version 20.1 of the Accounts Stored in Databases platform requires the corresponding 20.1 framework; the exact patched build for CA26-36 must still come from the bulletin. 
4. Production implementation
Perform the update on one CPM server at a time where the architecture permits.
1.	Confirm that no emergency password rotations or reconciliation activities are in progress. 
2.	Confirm that the downloaded package names and versions match CA26-36. 
3.	Import the updated platform or extension packages through: 
Administration
  > Platform Management
  > Import Platform
4.	Stop the CyberArk Password Manager service on the CPM being updated before manually replacing local executable or DLL files, where manual deployment is required. 
5.	Deploy the remediated files according to the Marketplace package instructions. 
6.	For the WMI plugin: 
o	Preserve the new patched files. 
o	Compare the previous command-file customizations. 
o	Merge only approved custom statements into the patched files. 
7.	Do not overwrite the full CPM bin directory. 
8.	Preserve the existing NTFS ownership and permissions. 
9.	Start the CyberArk Password Manager service. 
10.	Confirm that the service remains running and that the CPM reconnects successfully. 
11.	Verify the file versions and hashes. 
12.	Repeat the process on every CPM server. 
CyberArk also supports centrally deploying plugin DLLs through the PasswordManagerShared Safe. Updated plugins are then distributed when CPM configuration refreshes. Because the default refresh interval is documented as 24 hours, do not close the Change based only on the central upload—confirm that the corrected files are physically present on every CPM. 
Post-change validation
Complete all of the following before closing the Change:
1.	Confirm the CPM service is running on every server. 
2.	Confirm all affected files match the CA26-36 fixed versions. 
3.	Confirm no older vulnerable copies remain in: 
o	The CPM bin directory. 
o	Versioned plugin directories. 
o	Temporary deployment directories. 
o	PasswordManagerShared, where centrally deployed. 
4.	Run controlled tests: 
o	One TPC-based account: Verify and Change. 
o	One Windows Local WMI account: Change and Reconcile. 
o	One database-dependent account: Update the database credential. 
5.	Confirm that the new password is synchronized between the target and Vault. 
6.	Review PM.log and the relevant plugin logs for initialization, loading or dependency errors. 
7.	Review failed CPM activities after the maintenance window. 
8.	Confirm normal scheduled password-management operations resume. 
9.	Run the vulnerability scanner again and attach the clean scan results to the Change. 
10.	Retain the package versions, hashes, screenshots and test results as implementation evidence. 
Expected impact
During the restart of each CPM:
•	Password Verify, Change and Reconcile operations assigned to that CPM will temporarily pause. 
•	Pending CPM operations should resume after the CPM service becomes available. 
•	Password retrieval and normal privileged connection activity are not expected to depend directly on the CPM service, although application-specific workflows should still be tested. 
•	No Vault or PVWA outage is expected from the plugin-only remediation. 
CPM plugins are responsible for changing, verifying and reconciling passwords, which is why those operations are the primary affected functions during the maintenance. 
Rollback plan
Rollback should only be used if the remediated plugin causes service or password-management failures.
1.	Stop the CyberArk Password Manager service on the affected CPM. 
2.	Restore the backed-up plugin/framework files and their original NTFS permissions. 
3.	Restore the previous platform export if the platform configuration was changed. 
4.	Restore the previous WMI command files only as part of an approved emergency rollback. 
5.	Start the CyberArk Password Manager service. 
6.	Confirm the CPM reconnects successfully. 
7.	Run Verify/Change/Reconcile against the designated test accounts. 
8.	Confirm that passwords remain synchronized. 
9.	Open a high-severity CyberArk Support case referencing CA26-36. 
10.	Treat rollback as temporary because it restores the vulnerable component.

